# Connections have to be able to wait for accept(), because camenc calls it
# between frames rather than blocking on it: see camenc_ws.c, which refuses
# to build without this.  Without it a connection arriving while the encoder
# is busy is dropped, which looks like the network rather than the program.
CONFIG_NET_TCPBACKLOG=y

# And sends have to be able to come back still holding data.  Without write
# buffering NuttX sends inline and waits for the acknowledgement, and it does
# that whether or not the socket is non-blocking -- the non-blocking flag is
# simply not consulted on that path.  camenc's server queues a frame and
# expects send() to give back EAGAIN when the socket is full, so without this
# every frame waits for a WiFi round trip inside the capture loop, and the
# camera's rate drops from 62.5 fps to under 40.  See camenc_ws.c, which
# refuses to build without it.
CONFIG_NET_TCP_WRITE_BUFFERS=y

# The pool the write buffers are drawn from, and the reason neither number is
# the default.
#
# What the pool has to hold is set by the size of one frame, and that is the
# thing that moved.  This was sized when every frame was an IDR of about
# 3.5 KB, and ThreeFrames x 3.5 KB / 196 B is where sixty-four came from.
# With two pictures per group an IDR is a whole intra picture again -- about
# 16.5 KB in the same scene -- and the arithmetic that produced 64 now
# produces a pool smaller than a single frame.  A send() that cannot even
# accept one frame never finishes it, a client is fed at the rate the
# acknowledgements free buffers rather than the rate WiFi allows, and a
# viewer sees nothing at all while the capture loop reports its usual 62.5
# fps.
#
# So the payload is a packet's worth rather than a fifth of one, and the
# product is what matters: 1516 bytes is one full Ethernet payload, so the
# same 64 buffers now hold 97 KB -- six of today's frames, or more than a
# worst-case 64 KB segment -- for 85 KB more RAM than before.  Raising the
# count instead would work too, but it would take 85 buffers for a single
# frame and leave the pool unable to hold one; this way a buffer is always
# something that can be sent.
CONFIG_IOB_BUFSIZE=1516
CONFIG_IOB_NBUFFERS=64

# How much of that pool one connection may hold.
#
# The pool is shared, and until this was set nothing bounded a share of it:
# CONFIG_NET_SEND_BUFSIZE defaults to zero, which the stack reads as no limit,
# so a single connection could queue every buffer there was.  A viewer whose
# browser tab had been refreshed and whose socket had stopped draining did
# exactly that, and the arithmetic above says how little it takes -- the whole
# pool is 97 KB, and the frames are what they are.
#
# What made it fatal rather than merely unfair is that the receive path draws
# from the same pool.  With every buffer queued as unacknowledged transmit
# data, the stack could not receive the FIN that the peer had already sent,
# so the application never learned the connection was over: it went on
# queueing frames for a client that was gone, the buffers stayed queued, and
# every other client's send() returned EAGAIN for want of a free one.  A
# refresh was served, then another, and the third hung -- the page received
# nothing at all, because the way out of that state was a packet the stack had
# nowhere to put.
#
# 16 KB is one of today's frames, so a connection can queue what it is being
# given without waiting, and four of them can hold at most 43 of the 64
# buffers between them.  That leaves the receive path its share -- the
# throttle below keeps eight of them for it regardless -- and with that, a
# client that stops reading costs its own frames and nothing else, which is
# what the application's own drop-rather-than-wait policy already assumed.
CONFIG_NET_SEND_BUFSIZE=16384

# Letting go of a client without waiting for it.
#
# A client this server drops -- a viewer replaced by a refreshed page, a
# connection that has gone, a socket that will not take what is queued -- has
# nothing left to be delivered to it, but the data already in its socket has
# not been acknowledged and close() waits for that before finishing with the
# connection.  Nothing ever acknowledges it, so the connection is left
# retransmitting until the retransmission limit is reached, and it holds the
# write buffers it was given for the whole of that time: eight attempts with
# an RTO that starts at three seconds is minutes, not seconds.  Ten refreshes
# would put the pool back where this section started.
#
# SO_LINGER with a zero time is the abortive close POSIX defines for this: the
# queued data is discarded, the connection is reset, and its buffers are
# released when the reset is sent rather than when an acknowledgement that
# will never come fails to arrive.  camenc_ws.c refuses to build without it,
# because a client that is dropped gently leaves no mark on any number this
# application prints -- see the note there.
CONFIG_NET_SOLINGER=y

# And why a descriptor can fail to register in the first place.
#
# CONFIG_NET_PREALLOC_DEVIF_CALLBACKS is a fixed pool of socket event
# callbacks -- sixteen of them -- and with dynamic allocation off it is a hard
# ceiling rather than a starting point: devif_callback_alloc() draws from it
# with a zero timeout and has nowhere else to go, so once they are all held,
# every further registration fails and tcp_pollsetup() answers -EBUSY.
#
# That failure is not local to the descriptor that suffers it, because of how
# poll() is built here.  A caller that polls several descriptors loses the
# ones after the first failure: fs/vfs/fs_poll.c abandons them, marks the
# failing one POLLERR and returns immediately, so poll() never waits -- and
# the wait is the only time these callbacks are registered, which is the only
# way a reset or a peer's close is ever delivered to the application.  So an
# exhausted callback pool does not look like a shortage; it looks like a
# server that cannot tell when a viewer has gone, which is what it did look
# like for several days of this.
#
# A connected socket holds more than one: a connection monitor for as long as
# it is connected (tcp_monitor.c), and a send callback for as long as it has
# data queued and unacknowledged (tcp_send_buffered.c).  A viewer that has
# stopped reading holds both indefinitely, and a server that cannot see a
# reset never lets it go -- so four clients reach sixteen, the listening
# socket can no longer register either, and from then on every round reports
# it in error.  The pool has to be a function of the work rather than a
# ceiling.
#
# With 1, a callback is allocated when it is needed and returned when it is
# not.  (2 or more would allocate in batches and keep them; the board has the
# memory either way, but nothing here wants to hold it.)
CONFIG_NET_ALLOC_DEVIF_CALLBACKS=1

# And the same question once more, for the connections themselves.
#
# CONFIG_NET_TCP_PREALLOC_CONNS is eight, and with CONFIG_NET_TCP_ALLOC_CONNS
# at zero the Kconfig is explicit that eight is not a starting point: it is
# "the maximum number of connections available to the system at all times".
# A connection structure is held for the life of the connection and then,
# when this side closes first and closes gently, for the whole of TIME_WAIT.
# The page reply is closed that way deliberately -- its last bytes are still
# in the socket and a reset would throw them away -- so every page load
# spends one, and a browser brings connections of its own besides.
#
# It is worth being exact about what running out looks like, because it is
# not a refusal.  tcp_input() drops the SYN and sends nothing at all, not
# even a reset (nuttx/net/tcp/tcp_input.c, "No free TCP connections"); the
# peer retransmits and eventually gives up, which the browser reports as a
# connection timeout.  The server meanwhile reports no clients, no accepts
# and no errors of any kind, and its camera keeps running at full rate -- an
# application that is perfectly healthy while nothing can reach it.  That is
# a much harder thing to read than a server that is visibly in trouble, and
# it is the second time in this file that a pool with a fixed ceiling has
# produced it.
#
# So this pool has to be a function of the work as well.  Not a larger
# ceiling: TIME_WAIT is measured in tens of seconds and a browser opens
# several connections per page load, so any fixed number is merely further
# away, and the failure would come back looking the same.
# CONFIG_NET_TCP_MAX_CONNS is there if a limit is wanted, but it comes with
# that arithmetic.  (The UDP and ICMP pools are still ceilings -- eight and
# four -- which nothing in camenc uses; they are left alone.)
CONFIG_NET_TCP_ALLOC_CONNS=1

